mic Republic regardless of who's  president so President Ahmadinejad's has  something that they don't like or we  don't like the the problem is contained  in that there will be people who will  challenge him the next time around in  the next election so the first thing I  do is I draw from from my contacts  across the across the spectrum in Iran  which i think is very unique it will be  a new unique contribution to the debate  the others the other is of course going  through our archival material from each  of the u.s. administration's dating back  to 19th from 1979 to document the number  of times and the depth of interaction  constructive interaction many cases  between the United States and the  Islamic Republic so one of the other  myths is well the Islamic Republic is so  radical so hostile to the United States  and in fact means the hostility towards  United States to legitimate its own  political order one of the ways that I'm  going about I think deconstructing that  that myth and rebutting that myth is to  go back and show how every US  administration reached out to the  Islamic Republic of Iran to cooperate  with it in some way whether it was  President Reagan's administration with  what we know about iran-contra  iran-contra scandal the first George  Bush administration reached out to the  Islamic Republic to get Iran's help to  secure the release of us hostage  hostages in lebanon the Clinton  administration cooperated in directly  with the Iranians to supply the Muslims  in Bosnia with weapons during the Balkan  conflict in the 1990s and of course the  the Bush administration that I worked  for cooperated with sought Iran's help  for Afghanistan and al-qaeda in each one  of these cases we go through the all the  archival material and you interview  people in each one of these cases of  cooperation it was the united states  that cut off the cooperation for reasons  that had nothing to do with iran for  reasons that had to do with our domestic  politics f