support the Islamic Republic even  if they don't necessarily support the  person in President Ahmadinejad I would  argue that the vast majority still  support the idea of the Islamic Republic  let me give you an example of one of the  things that I found in doing just doing  the research and drawing on my personal  experiences no matter how many books  people read on Iran rarely does it come  out that the political in the political  system in Iran the president is  term-limited but this is something in my  discussions with Iranian officials that  would come out whether it was dealing  with President the issue of President  Ahmadinejad or before him president  taught to me who was a more of a  reformist figure seen as a little bit  more in line with Western ideas of  modernization in both cases Iranian  diplomats would talk about Iranian  officials from across the political  spectrum would talk about the national  interest for the Islamic Republic and if  someone would mention well but President  Ahmadinejad said this or president ought  to me said this there was all due  deference to their head of state but  there was a there was it was clear for  them and they would say it repeatedly  well yes he is important but the system  does not rest on one person whether it's  the president who is term-limited will  even the supreme leader that one of the  things that's interesting and important  about the system inside the Islamic  Republic almost like the system inside  China is that both the founding father  of the Islamic Republic Ayatollah  Khomeini as well as chairman mao in  China they both bequeathed to their  systems not not not into their countries  not a western-style democratic system  but a competitive political system that  endures beyond personalities so what I  try to bring from my personal experience  in talking to so many different Iranian  officials and people who live in Iran's  support the Islamic Republic is this  idea of the enduring nature of the  Islamic Republic regardless of who's  president so President Ahmadinejad's has  something that they don't like or we  don't like the the problem is contained  in that there will be people who will  challenge him the next time around in  the next election so the first thing I  do is I draw from from my contacts  across the across the spectrum in Iran  which i think is very unique it will be  a new unique contribution to the debate  the others the other is of course going  through our archival material from each  of the u.s. administration's dating back  to 19th from 1979 to document the number  of times and the depth of interaction  constructive interaction many cases  between the United States and the  Islamic Republic so one of the other  myths is well the Islamic Republic is so  radical so hostile to the United States  and in fact means the hostility towards  United States to legitimate its own  political order one of the ways that I'm  going about I think deconstructing that  that myth and rebutting that myth is to  go back and show how every US  administration reached out to the  Islamic Republic of Iran to cooperate  with it in some way whether it was  President Reagan's administration with  what we know about iran-contra  iran-contra scandal the first George  Bush administration reached out to the  Islamic Republic to get Iran's help to  secure the release of us hostage  hostages in lebanon the Clinton  administration cooperated in directly  with the Iranians to supply the Muslims  in Bosnia with weapons during the Balkan  conflict in the 1990s and of course the  the Bush administration that I worked  for cooperated with sought Iran's help  for Afghanistan and al-qaeda in each one  of these cases we go through the all the  archival material and you interview  people in each one of these cases of  cooperation it was the united states  that cut off the cooperation for reasons  that had nothing to do with iran for  reasons that had to do with our domestic  politics for the most part in each one  of these cases the Iranians delivered  not everything the United States asked  for but almost everything they delivered  a lot of what we asked for it was the  United States so that cut off each one  of these periods of engagement going  back to iran contra iran-contra failed  not because the Iranians didn't come  through but because of a constitutional  crisis here in the United States when  Reagan administration officials try to  divert the proceeds from selling weapons  to the Iranians to fund the Contras in  Nicaragua in contravention with with US  law so we've had this you know this  really messy situation on the US side  undermine cooperation throughout so  that's the other that's the other major  area I'll be drawing I'll be drawing on  I am very curious and listening to you  to know whether there are any gender  barriers when you're talking with  Iranian Iranian officials as a woman did  you have any difficulty I had no  difficulty once  ever it's i think that the inside the  islamic republic i think this is one of  the other myths that it's a misogynistic  political water i think this is really a  myth before the Islamic Revolution women  were able to of course walk the streets  without their hair covered and there was  this idea of kind of a liberal  atmosphere for women but in fact many  women couldn't really go to school or  hold senior government jobs or senior  jobs in business or the professions now  many of that was but most of that as  much of that was almost self selective  conservative families didn't really  weren't that interested in having women  particularly from villages and non-urban  areas having their daughters or their  wives go to university to mingle you  know to be with people the opposite sex  or to be in positions in the office  where they're you know they're  vulnerable innocence now of course many  women before the Islamic Republic was  created did benefit if they were in the  elite but if he weren't in the elite in  Iran if you're a woman and most of the  population were not elite you really had  a problem in terms of educational and  career advancement what the Islamic  Republic has done is really even the  field so while elite women of course  can't go out cover there's you have to  be covered in in the streets and in the  professions there is there the system  for education and for the professions  really essentially opened for the first  time to people outside of northern  tehran which is the most cosmopolitan  part of iran in a way where if you look  at the statistics the unesco statistics  and other you know international  statistics in terms of literacy rates  and participation of women in many  different sectors the advancement of  women over the past 30 years has been  strikingly significant inside iran I can  you know even tell you from my personal  experience of being there it's you know  you look out the window and you'll see  in a car you look out the window that  there's a woman driving a minibus so  there are women who you know there's  whose women who are ministers in the  government women who's driving a woman  who is driving a bus at the University  one of the big problems is that there  are so many women there they're  concerned that there aren't there  there's going to be such a  disproportionate number of women that  they will outnumber the men and they may  need to institute an affirmative action  trivet of action program for men it also  becomes the Islamic Republic does a lot  of very interesting things the other one  of the other interesting things it's  done is it's one of the most advanced  countries in terms of family planning  initially in the early days of the  revolution the revolutionaries  encouraged large families as part of  their needs i said for greater  population the Islamic Republic but very  soon they realized what kind of you know  the destructive aspects of this for for  Iranians for modernization and just for  the for the for the country and they  instituted a pretty comprehensive family  planning program where the country today  it's it's pretty easy to get family  planning advice and assistance and  there's also a lot of emphasis on things  that that we've come to relatively you  know well it's relatively late i think  in terms of embracing women to  breastfeed their children to have places  in the workplace to do so i often travel  with my young children and i find you  know the islamic republic is one of one  of the few places where it's easy to  bring them in there's an understanding  about women in the workplace also having  children and having this this this  balance I don't say that to say that  it's any kind of ideal place it is not  by any stretch but I don't think that  there's I don't think there are many  places that are ideal but i think this  there it is a myth that it's a really  misogynistic place there's a lot  tremendous room for improvement I think  Iranians of all genders and walks of  life are constantly working to make it a  better place there are many many areas  that need improvement but it's I think  part of a caricature that just misses a  lot of the struggle that women have in  all societies they struggle in Iran as  well but there are a lot of there have  also been some very tangible benefits  over the past past 30 years very good  let's talk a little bit about some of  the findings in your book I know it's a  bit premature and that it's it's not  completed yet but can you share some of  them with us I think the some of the the  most important findings are how  difficult it will be particularly on the  US side to really transform the  relationship to come to grips with the  Islamic Republic that it will take a  tremendous amount of almost re-education  on our parts to really come to terms  with what goes on inside the Islamic  Republic and to take a much more to take  a really sober look that their foreign  policy and national security strategy  the other pieces how how difficult it  will be to manage deal with our allies  in the region so that a realignment  between the United States and the  Islamic Republic of Iran is not  perceived as a zero-sum game against our  allies so in that regard to look at to  look at Israel to look at saudi arabia  and how to one of the important findings  is that there's a tremendous amount of  concern within Saudi Arabia within  Israel US allies of how the u.s. could  turn the relationship with Iran the  necessity to do so now the necessity to  do so takes almost two extremes the  necessity to do so is if the United  States could somehow get its act  together and figure out a way to deal  constructively with the Islamic Republic  very few people in the Middle East think  that we can do that the other extreme is  how are we then going to militarily take  care of this problem with the Islamic  Republic so that's that's the major  those are I think the major difficulties  and how the United States is going to  deal with with our allies in the region  to make any kind of realignment in  relations not a zero-sum game and so I'd  say that's probably one of the the most  disturbing most challenging findings in  the research so far as how entrenched  views are not only in the United States  but in the region in terms of how to  deal with with the rise of Iran very  thanks so much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your work  thank you very much for more information  about professor Leverett and her work  please visit our website at yale edu  backslash Macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale
 

